  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nHigh pressure remained in control across the Northland early\nthis afternoon with mainly clear skies across the region.\nEasterly winds off Lake Superior will again be gusty this\nafternoon, keeping a risk for rip currents in place another day\nalong Minnesota and Wisconsin Points. Another cool night is\nexpected under clear skies along with light winds. Lows were\nadjusted slightly downward with this update with 30s expected\nfor most with 40s near Lake Superior. Another Frost Advisory is\nlikely for tonight, but will likely not be as expansive as the\none last night.\n\nAs the high slides a bit more to the east tonight into tomorrow,\nwinds will turn more southerly. Highs tomorrow will be slightly\nwarmer than today, with lower to middle 60s for most along with\nsome 50s along the North Shore with onshore flow. Winds in the\nwestern arm of the lake will be more easterly with terrain\nfunneling effects and lead elevated risk for rip currents at\nTwin Port beaches.\n\nAs upper ridging starts to build in tomorrow, a mid-level\nshortwave will move from the Northern Plains into the Upper\nMidwest tomorrow afternoon into tomorrow night. This will bring\nchances for showers mainly along and east of US 53/I-35 in\nMinnesota tomorrow night into Thursday. This system will be\nrunning into the surface high to the east and drier air in place\nand is expected to weaken as it crosses. Much of northwest\nWisconsin may not see anything with this shortwave. \n\nSoutherly flow is again expected for Friday, allowing highs to\nwarm into the 60s with a few lower 70s in spots. Another\nshortwave will lift along the periphery of the upper ridge\nFriday into the weekend as the ridge axis shifts eastward. This\nwill bring another chance for showers to the region mainly on\nSaturday. Some of the models are a little more excited in terms\nof QPF with this system, but given the drought in place and\noverall setup, it doesn't instill much confidence in getting\nthose higher amounts. Temperatures remain in the 60s with some\nlow 70s in spots through the weekend and into early next week\nwith additional chances for rainfall as the pattern becomes a\nbit more active.",
